    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3f6649b1-a9d3-54e2-874a-3cb2e93fc349",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "The average gas price in Chicago is $\\$3.93$ per gallon. Gas prices have been *growing* continuously at a rate of $2.8\\%$ per month. What was the gas price $8$ months ago? Round your answer to the nearest cent.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the continuously compounded growth equation $P=P_0e^{rt}$. The current amount is $P=3.93$, the rate is $r=0.028$ (positive for growth), and the elapsed time is $t=8$ months. The time unit matches the rate. Substitute and divide by the exponential factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.93 &= P_0e^{(0.028)(8)} \\\\\n3.93 &= P_0e^{0.224} \\\\\ne^{0.224} &\\approx 1.25107102 \\\\\nP_0 &= \\frac{3.93}{e^{0.224}} \\\\\n&\\approx 3.14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe earlier gas price was $\\$3.14$ per gallon. Round only the final amount; keep full calculator precision in the exponential factor.",
      ":question/requires-calculator": true,
